rner</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 SF are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to SEA \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Jadarian Price</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>SEA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>SEA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jadarian Price</strong> (13 carries, 54 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though SEA's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 75 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>AJ Barner</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 26 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>SF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>SF are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 SF project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 75 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Mike Evans</strong> is the one to target from a volume 